From e6f23baf5edb2681857f3c4ee4da3a5705aec32f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cory Forsythe <cory@coryforsythe.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:04:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 18/24] Fix uncaught socket exceptions that could crash the
 hermes-vps bridge silently

_WSSocket wrote/read the raw socket directly with no exception
translation, so a real connection failure (dropped VPS connectivity,
a reset, an SSL error) raised an unwrapped OSError instead of
HermesWsError. Every caller up the stack (rpc, Bridge._forward,
Bridge.run's main loop, main()) only ever handled HermesWsError, so
the exception crashed the whole bridge process uncaught - no
write_status_line() call, no blocked: signal, exactly the silent
failure mode this bridge exists to prevent.

Route every raw socket write/read through new _send()/_recv() helpers
that translate OSError to HermesWsError, matching what every caller
already expected. Live-verified the crash against a refused
connection before the fix and confirmed the new regression test
catches it.

Investigated after a captain report that typing directly into a live
hermes-vps pane and pressing Enter produced no response. Extensive
falsification testing (character-by-character keystroke delivery, a
literal embedded CR with no LF, bulk paste + separate Enter) could not
reproduce that exact failure through any interactive path reachable
via the pane API - all round-tripped correctly through the live VPS.
This fix addresses a real, independently-found defect consistent with
the report (a stale connection crashing the bridge with no visible
pane signal), but the root cause of the captain's specific report is
not confirmed; see docs/verification/hermes.md for the full
investigation trail and what remains open.
